27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gpt-6-astra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.029 3.747KB 9.71s
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #It’s a straightforward WebSocket client: connect, initialise the terminal, send ticks, and
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #reconnect if the connection drops. The named event handlers make it easy to follow.
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #The main things I’d watch:
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #- Possible echo loop. `onMessage()` sends an `"echo"` request for every incoming
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #message—including JSON-RPC responses. If the server replies to each echo, that creates a continuous
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #back-and-forth. Distinguish requests, notifications, and responses before deciding what to send.
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #- Ticks start before the connection opens. Early ticks will be discarded, but `count` still
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #advances. If you want the server to receive all ticks, start sending after connection—or after the
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #`"init"` response, if initialisation needs acknowledgement.
27a4ab71 [gpt-6-astra] #- JSON parsing can throw. Malformed messages will cause an uncaught exception in the
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #handler. Also, `event.data` is only guaranteed to be text if the server sends text frames; binary
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #frames need separate handling.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- JSON-RPC responses aren’t tracked. Every outgoing message has an `id`, which makes it a
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #request expecting a response. If `"tick"` is fire-and-forget, consider making it a notification by
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #omitting `id`, assuming the server supports that.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- Reconnect has no shutdown path. The one-second retry is fine for local development, but
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #an intentional close would also reconnect. A stop/dispose method and a reconnect timer you can
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #cancel would help.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #A few smaller details:
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- With TypeScript’s `strictPropertyInitialization` enabled, `ws` will be flagged because the
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #constructor assigns it indirectly through `connect()`.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- The loop attempts 101 ticks, with counts from `0` through `100`.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- Ending the loop doesn’t close the socket.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- `readonly OPEN = 1` is unused.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- `StartgClient` would be the conventional class name; `const payload` and a more specific type
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #than `any` would tighten things up.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #- Top-level `await` requires a compatible module/runtime setup.
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #
27a4ab72 [gpt-6-astra] #• Overall:* a useful local prototype. I’d address message routing and connection readiness
27a4ab73 [gpt-6-astra] #first; those are the most likely sources of surprising behaviour.
27a4ab73 [gpt-6-astra] #
