27a4f458 [glm-5.3] #Content outline:
27a4f458 [glm-5.3] #1. Origins: Buenos Aires, Argentina; Juan "reduz" Linietsky and Ariel "punto-" Manzur; started ~2001/2004 as in-house tool for client work; used commercially for ~a decade before open sourcing; hence "Waiting for Godot" name — joke that it was always "almost ready"/they were waiting for the right moment. Actually the canonical name story: "we were waiting for the publisher's approval"? Let me settle: I've read the FAQ entry: "Why the name 'Godot'?" — answer along lines: "The name was suggested by a friend... after Beckett's play. It fits because the engine was in development for a long time and we were always 'waiting' for it to be done." There's also the anecdote that it referenced waiting for a publisher to pick up a game. I'll present the Beckett reference + the self-deprecating joke, without inventing precise details.
27a4f458 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f458 [glm-5.3] #2. Open sourcing: February 2014, MIT license, on GitHub. Rémi Verschelde (akien) joined soon after as project manager. 1.0 stable December 2014. Joined Software Freedom Conservancy later that year — hmm, was it later that year? I said November 2014 earlier; I believe the Conservancy announcement was 2014-11 or 2015-02. Hedge: "soon after".
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #3. Growth: 2.x era community growth; 2015 Kickstarter (~$20k) funding 3.0; Godot 3.0 (Jan 2018) — big graphics overhaul, PBR, GDNative, C# support came in 3.0 (Mono integration by Neikeq). Actually C# support shipped in 3.0 (2018). Yes, 3.0 included the Mono/C# module.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #4. Godot 4.0: years in the making, released March 1, 2023; Vulkan, GDScript 2.0, GDExtension, physics rewrite (Godot Physics), etc.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #5. Unity exodus Sept 2023: Unity's runtime fee announcement drove huge adoption spike — GitHub stars surged, funding jumped; community (including e.g. many devs switching). Later Unity partially walked back, then cancelled entirely (Unity cancelled runtime fees Sept 2024 under new CEO). Maybe skip Unity's reversal details.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #6. Stewardship: Godot Foundation established (2024) to own/steer the project, with development funded by community donations and corporate sponsors.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #7. Design identity: node/scene tree, editor built in Godot itself, GDScript created by Juan; MIT keeps it friendly for proprietary/commercial use — relevant since user is using it as a display terminal app.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #Also could mention: mascot robot; "Made with Godot" games: Brotato, Dome Keeper, Cassette Beasts, Cruelty Squad, Ex Zodiac, Halls of Torment, Buckshot Roulette, Windblown (early access 2025?)... Notable: "Sonic Colors: Ultimate" used a fork (Godot-based? Yes — Sonic Colors Ultimate's "Godot Engine" fork; built by Blind Squirrel Games using a licensed/modified Godot). Maybe mention a couple of standout titles briefly. Also noteworthy: the engine powers non-game uses — like the user's terminal display. Nice tie-in.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #Keep length moderate. Use a timeline-ish structure with bold years. Avoid overly long lists. Maybe ~300-450 words. Format: short intro line, then a few compact paragraphs grouped by era with bolded era markers, ending with a one-line tie-in to their project. No code.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #Accuracy checks on specific claims:
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Juan Linietsky: yes, Argentine, nickname "reduz".
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Ariel Manzur: yes, nickname "punto".
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Started: sources vary; commonly "developed since 2001" (Wikipedia says development began in 2001). Some say Juan started his engine work in 2001, and the two combined efforts around 2004? Wikipedia's line: "It was initially developed... by Juan Linietsky and Ariel Manzur... before being released to the public in 2014." And "Godot's development began... in 2001". I'll say "work traces back to the early 2000s (~2001)".
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Used for client work: yes, they ran companies doing contract software/games; engine used for "many published products" over ~a decade.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Open sourced Feb 2014 on GitHub, MIT. Yes.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- 1.0 stable: December 2014. Yes.
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Conservancy: announced November 2014 (I'm fairly confident; there's a blog "Godot joins the Software Freedom Conservancy"). I'll say "later in 2014".
27a4f459 [glm-5.3] #- Kickstarter: 2015, goal $20k. I'll say "a small 2015 Kickstarter (goal ~$20k) to fund what became 3.0".
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- Godot 3.0: January 29, 2018. Yes.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- C# in 3.0: yes (experimental Mono module).
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- 4.0: March 1, 2023. Yes.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- Unity runtime fee: Sept 12, 2023. Godot saw record interest. Yes.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- Godot Foundation: I'm fairly sure the announcement of establishing the Godot Foundation was around August 2024 ("The Godot Foundation is born" / "Godot becomes a foundation" — there was a blog post). I'll say "in 2024" — reasonably confident.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- 4.4: March 2025. 4.5: September 2025 — I believe released Sept 15, 2025. I'll phrase softly: "4.5 landed in 2025".
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- Famous games: Dome Keeper, Brotato, Cassette Beasts, Cruelty Squad, Buckshot Roulette, Halls of Torment; Sonic Colors Ultimate used a licensed fork. I can cite two or three.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- Editor built with Godot itself: yes.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #- Name: Waiting for Godot by Beckett — yes; the joke about the engine perpetually in development / waiting. Also pronunciation debates — skip.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #One more nice fact: they open-sourced because mobile/console shifts made engine licensing deals unattractive and they wanted... The stated reason in interviews: after years of in-house use, they felt they couldn't compete with Unity/Unreal as a product, but believed in FOSS; also motivations around the state of libre game tech (they wanted a truly free engine). Rémi's account: they released it hoping a community would form. Keep it brief.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #Format: I'll write a narrative with a handful of bolded milestones inline or short paragraphs. Given the user's casual question, a clean prose answer with a compact timeline works. I'll avoid headers; use paragraphs + maybe one short list. End with a light tie-in sentence about their websocket terminal being a typical "Godot as an app platform" use. Keep courteous, not patronising.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #=================
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #The short version: it's a two-person Argentine side project that spent over a decade as an in-house
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #tool before anyone outside could use it.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #• The origins (early 2000s). Juan Linietsky and Ariel Manzur, two developers from
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #Buenos Aires, started building the engine around 2001 for their own contract work — games and
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #applications for clients across Latin America. It was never a product; it was the private toolchain
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #they shipped commercial projects with for roughly a decade. The name comes from Beckett's
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #Waiting for Godot* — partly an in-joke that the engine had been "almost finished" for years.
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45a [glm-5.3] #• Open sourcing (2014).* They released it on GitHub in February 2014 under the MIT licence,
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #essentially as a leap of faith — they didn't expect it to compete with Unity or Unreal, but wanted
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #a genuinely free engine to exist. Rémi Verschelde joined as project manager early on and became the
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #organisational backbone. Version 1.0 stable arrived that December, and the project joined the
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #Software Freedom Conservancy shortly after.
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #• The 3.0 era (2015–2018).* A modest 2015 Kickstarter (~$20k goal) funded the graphics
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #overhaul that became Godot 3.0 in January 2018 — PBR rendering, GDNative, and experimental C#
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #support. This is when the community started growing seriously.
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #• Godot 4 and the Unity exodus (2023). Version 4.0, released March 2023 after years of
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #rewrites, brought Vulkan, GDScript 2.0, and GDExtension. Then in September 2023 Unity announced its
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #runtime fee, and Godot experienced probably the largest single adoption spike of any engine: a
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #flood of GitHub stars, downloads, and donations overnight. Titles like Dome Keeper,
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #Brotato, and Cassette Beasts* had already proven it commercially. The project later
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #established its own Godot Foundation in 2024 to take over stewardship from the Conservancy, and the
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #4.x line has been shipping steadily since (4.4 in early 2025, 4.5 later in 2025).
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #A couple of things that make it distinctive: the editor is itself built in Godot, and the
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #node/scene-tree architecture leans toward composability rather than inheritance. That's also why it
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #works well outside games — using it as a JSON-RPC-driven display terminal, as you're doing, is a
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #fairly common "Godot as an app platform" pattern.
27a4f45b [glm-5.3] #
