280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #Let me also double-check the base64 encode - it looks like it might be non-standard. The comment says "Encode is RFC3501 json friendly" - RFC3501 is IMAP4rev1 which uses base64 with + and , ... hmm, actually RFC3501 section 5.1.3 mentions base64 but not with comma. The Encode64 table has `+,=` at the end instead of `+/=`. So it's a modified base64. Decode uses `+/=`. So encode and decode are asymmetric! Encoding uses `+` for index 62 and `,` for index 63, but decoding uses `+` for 62 and `/` for 63. That means round-tripping through the "verify" in `base64()` would fail... but wait, the verify uses `decodeBase64(result)` where result is the encoded form. If the encoded form contains `,`, the decoder will fail to find it in Decode64 (find returns npos which is huge, not 64). Actually `str64.find(b[p])` returns `std::string::npos` which is SIZE_MAX, not 64. So `i0<<2` would be garbage. Hmm, but only if `,` appears. And if `/` appears in encoded (it won't from encode since encode uses `,`). So there's an asymmetry bug.
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #Actually let me re-read: `Encode64="...+,="` and `Decode64="...+/="`. So they differ at position 63: encode uses `,` decode uses `/`. So any input producing index 63 would encode to `,` but decode wouldn't recognize it. That's a real bug (or intentional modification not matched).
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #OK. Let me also note `base64()` function has an unused variable `result` returned - actually it returns `result`. But `utf8 byte=code.substr(2,2);` is dead code.
