280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #Also the bit shifting in encode:
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #```cpp
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #int shift=bit&7;
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #uint8_t sixbits=(((b0<<shift)>>2)|(b1>>(10-shift)))&0x3f;
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #```
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #For shift=0: `(b0>>2) | (b1>>10)` - b1>>10 is 0 for 8-bit. OK.
280d7bc5 [deepseek-flash] #For shift=6: `((b0<<6)>>2) | (b1>>4)` = `(b0<<4) | (b1>>4)`. b0<<4 as int... b0 is uint8_t promoted to int. `(b0<<6)` could be up to 255*64=16320, >>2 = 4080, &0x3f = 4080 & 63 = 48. Hmm, that's using low 6 bits of the 14-bit... `(b0<<6)>>2` = b0<<4. b0<<4 & 0x3f gives bits 0-3 of b0 shifted to bits 4-7? No: b0<<4 in 8 bits gives low nibble of b0 in high nibble. &0x3f masks to low 6 bits, giving bits 4-5 of that... this is getting complex. I'll just note it as "worth verifying with test vectors" rather than claim it's wrong.
