280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- Short answer: yes, several, one is remotely triggerable.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 1. The buffer overflow (worst).
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 2. The fd race / shutdown vs close.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 3. Unjoined threads.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 4. The spin.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 5. Leak + server exit on pthread_create failure.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 6. exit() with live threads.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #- 7. What's actually OK (ownership, double-close prevention) — to be fair and accurate.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #Keep it tight. No excessive code. Use prose. Maybe one small snippet for the shutdown pattern.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #Let me make sure I attribute correctly. `MaxRequest` is 240000.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #I'll write it now.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #=================
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #Yes — several, and the first one is remotely triggerable. I'll start with what's actually OK so the
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #rest is in context.
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #What holds up
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #The ownership model is sound by accident of lock ordering. The worker thread owns the
280d8829 [deepseek-flash] #object and is the only thing that `delete`s it; `closeHttpTasks` only ever touches connections
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #still present in `HttpTasks`, and it holds `taskMutex` for the whole iteration. Because the
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #worker's `closeHandle()` sits after the `remove()` (which needs the lock), a connection
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #cannot be closed by both `closeHttpTasks` and the worker simultaneously — the worker either removes
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #it first (so main never sees it) or blocks on the mutex until main is done. So the obvious
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #double-`close(fd)` is avoided. It's fragile, but it works.
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #Everything below is on top of that.
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #1. Heap overflow in `onRequest` — the serious one
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #size_t start=ss.tellg();
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #n-=start;
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #while(n<contentLength){
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #	ssize_t nn=recv(fd,readBuffer+start+n,contentLength-n,0);
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #`readBuffer` is a fixed `char[MaxRequest]` (240000) member. `start` is derived from the client's
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #header block and `contentLength` comes straight from the client's `Content-Length` header. Nothing
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #checks `start + contentLength <= MaxRequest`. A request with a few hundred KB of headers followed
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #by `Content-Length: 2000000` walks straight off the end of the member array and corrupts the heap.
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #The object is heap-allocated (`new HttpConnection`), so this is a straightforward remote heap
280d882a [deepseek-flash] #overflow. The subsequent `content=utf8(readBuffer+start,contentLength)` is the same problem in the
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #read direction.
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #This needs a bound check before the copy loop, and a cap on acceptable `Content-Length` (or a
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #streaming read into a growing buffer).
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #2. `fd` is raced, and `close` doesn't do what you want
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #`closeHttpTasks` calls `closeHandle()`, which does `close(fd); fd=-1;` from the main thread while
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #the worker may be inside `recv`/`send` on that same descriptor.
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #- Data race on the member. `fd` is a plain `int` read by the worker and written by main
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #with no synchronisation. That's UB; nothing stops the compiler from caching the load across the
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #loop.
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #- TOCTOU on reuse. The worker checks `if(fd==-1) return false;`, then calls `recv(fd,...)`.
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #Between those two, main can close the fd and a fresh `accept()` can hand the same number to a new
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #connection. The worker then reads or writes another client's socket.
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #- `close()` doesn't wake a blocked `recv`. On Linux the open file description survives
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #while a syscall holds a reference, so the worker stays parked until data arrives or the 3-second
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #`SO_RCVTIMEO` fires. Your shutdown isn't prompt, and during that window the fd number is free for
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #reuse.
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #
280d882b [deepseek-flash] #You already do this correctly for the listening sockets in `signalHandler`
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #(`shutdown(HttpSocket4, SHUT_RD)` then `close`). The per-connection sockets only get `close`. They
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #need the same treatment:
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #shutdown(fd, SHUT_RDWR);	// wakes a blocked recv with EOF
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #close(fd);
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #...and ideally the `fd` member should be atomic, or the shutdown/close should be serialised against
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #the worker via the same `taskMutex` you already use for the list.
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #3. Threads are never joined and never detached
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #`pthread_t httpThread` is a local in the accept loop. It's created joinable and then abandoned. On
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #glibc a joinable thread that exits keeps its descriptor (and, after the stack cache is exhausted,
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #its stack) allocated until someone joins it. So every request leaks. `pthread_detach` right after
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #`pthread_create` — or `pthread_attr_setdetachstate` — is the one-line fix.
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #Relatedly, there's no cap on concurrent connections. Each one is ~240 KB of object plus a thread.
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #`HttpTaskCount` is tracked and printed but never used for backpressure. A few thousand idle
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #keep-alives will exhaust memory and thread slots.
280d882c [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #4. Infinite spin on a half-closed body
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #if(nn==0){
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #	std::cout << "onRequest connection closed before contentLength read" << std::endl;
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #}
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #n+=nn;
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #If the peer sends headers with a large `Content-Length` and then half-closes, `recv` returns 0
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #forever. `n` never advances, the loop never exits, and the thread burns CPU permanently. (A
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #`SO_RCVTIMEO` expiry gives `-1`, which is handled — it's the clean FIN case that isn't.)
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #`nn==0` should `return false`.
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #5. `pthread_create` failure leaks and kills the server
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #HttpConnection *connection=new HttpConnection(platform,registry,client,fd2);
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #...
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #if(pthreadError){
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #	std::cout << "hostIPv4 pthread_create failure " << pthreadError << std::endl;
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #	return 4;
280d882d [deepseek-flash] #}
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #Both `connection` and `fd2` leak, and the `return` unwinds out of `hostIPv4` entirely — one
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #transient thread-creation failure takes the whole listener down. Under the connection-flood
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #scenario in (3) this is exactly the failure you'll hit first, and it's the one that should be
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #recoverable.
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #6. `exit()` while workers are still live
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #`signalHandler` calls `closeHttpTasks()`, sleeps one second, then `exit(signum)`. `exit` runs
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #static destructors, which destroys `HttpTasks` and `taskMutex` — both namespace-scope globals. Any
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #worker still in `onRequest` (see the 3-second window in (2)) can then lock a destroyed mutex or
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #walk a destroyed list. You need to join or wait for the workers before returning from the handler,
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #and even then the handler is doing non-async-signal-safe work (`std::cout`, `sleep_for`,
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #`closeHttpTasks`).
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #7. Minor
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #- `~HttpConnection` only warns if `fd > -1`; it never closes. Harmless today because the
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #worker always calls `closeHandle` first, but it means a construction failure path leaks the
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #descriptor silently.
280d882e [deepseek-flash] #- `HttpTasks`, `taskMutex` and `HttpTaskCount` are defined at namespace scope in a header. Fine
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #while `fit3.cpp` is the only TU including it; two TUs and you get separate copies, at which point
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #`closeHttpTasks` and `httpTask` would be operating on different lists.
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #- `size_t start = ss.tellg();` — if the header loop terminates on EOF rather than the blank line,
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #`tellg()` returns `-1`, which becomes `SIZE_MAX` and turns `n -= start` into `n += 1`. It's only
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #reachable with a malformed request (and only when `Content-Length > 0`), but it feeds directly into
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #the pointer arithmetic in (1), so it's worth guarding at the same time.
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #Priority
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #Fix (1) first — it's the only one that's a memory-safety bug reachable by a single crafted request.
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #Then (2), because it's the one that turns a benign "close from another thread" into
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #cross-connection data leakage. (3) and (4) are availability issues that a single client can
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #trigger; (5) and (6) are what turn those into a full outage.
280d882f [deepseek-flash] #
